(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cutaneous-viral-and-fungal-infections-vignettes"]={"title":"Cutaneous Viral and Fungal Infections Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 60-year-old man has diffuse thickening and scaling of both soles extending onto the medial and lateral surfaces of the feet, in a pattern resembling a shoe. Which variant of tinea pedis is this?","choices":["Tinea manuum","Hyperkeratotic","Interdigital","Vesiculobullous"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the shoe distribution is characteristic of the hyperkeratotic variant. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 19-year-old lifeguard has multiple velvety tan and white finely scaling macules across his upper trunk and neck. They are asymptomatic. Which organism is responsible, and is he contagious?","choices":["Overgrowth of lipid-dependent Malassezia species acquired from communal showers; it is considered contagious","Overgrowth of lipid-dependent Malassezia species that normally inhabit the skin; it is not considered contagious","Infection with Trichophyton rubrum acquired from another person; it is considered contagious","Infection with Candida albicans acquired from the patient's own gut flora; it is not considered contagious"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a normal resident overgrowing, so it does not spread person to person. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 24-year-old has a first episode of painful grouped vesicles on an erythematous base on the vulva, which have broken down into shallow ulcers. She has fever, headache and tender inguinal nodes. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chancroid ulceration","Vulvovaginal candidiasis","Herpes simplex virus infection","Primary syphilis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — grouped vesicles becoming shallow painful ulcers, with a systemic prodrome. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 26-year-old develops an intensely itchy papulovesicular eruption on the fingers about ten days after being treated for a foot infection. Careful examination shows an asymptomatic fissure and maceration in the toe webs. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A dermatophytid (id) reaction","Tinea manuum","Vesiculobullous tinea pedis","Herpetic whitlow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a distant inflammatory dermatitis, one to two weeks after the primary infection. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 55-year-old labourer has a thickened, dry, scaly right palm. He assumed it was from hard work. His left foot has interdigital scaling. Which term describes this pattern?","choices":["Two feet–one hand syndrome, in which the hand used to scratch the foot becomes affected","Id reaction, in which a dermatitis appears at a site distant from the primary infection","Tinea incognito, in which topical steroids alter the clinical appearance","Mosaic pattern, in which lesions cluster together on the weight-bearing surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and patients often blame dry skin or manual labour. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 21-year-old asks why his pityriasis versicolor keeps returning each summer. Which factors make it more common?","choices":["Cold, dry air, low sebum production, and frequent bathing with antibacterial soap","Obesity, incontinence, immobility, and recent antibiotic use in hospital","Occlusive footwear, nail trauma, and peripheral vascular disease","Heat, humidity, oily skin, sweating, immunosuppression, or corticosteroid exposure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — six named factors. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A man with a pustular beard eruption has hairs that are firmly anchored and do not pull out easily. Which test helps separate this from tinea barbae?","choices":["Patch testing","Serology for dermatophyte antibodies","Bacterial culture","Wood lamp examination"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — used to rule out bacterial folliculitis. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A patient with extensive, recurrent pityriasis versicolor has failed topical therapy. Why is oral terbinafine NOT an appropriate choice?","choices":["Because it is off label in the United States for any superficial fungal infection","Because adequate drug levels are not achieved in sweat, although topical terbinafine can be effective","Because it is ineffective against Malassezia by any route, including topically","Because it carries an unacceptable risk of hepatic and adrenal toxicity in superficial infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the route matters for this organism. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Six weeks into oral therapy for toenail onychomycosis, a patient is discouraged that the nail still looks abnormal. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["The therapy has failed, so it should be stopped and a topical agent substituted","The nail should look normal by now, so the original diagnosis was probably wrong","Improvement requires nail growth, so the course should be extended to two years","Improvement requires nail growth, so the appearance lags behind the treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — and any coexisting tinea pedis must also be managed. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A patient has an asymptomatic plantar wart found incidentally. Which is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Immediate surgical excision to prevent it clustering into a mosaic wart","Salicylic acid 40% daily for twelve weeks in every patient found to have one","No therapy is required, since plantar warts need treatment only when painful","Cryotherapy every two to three weeks until the lesion has fully resolved"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and many warts resolve spontaneously. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 145"},{"q":"A patient with tinea incognito has extensive, follicular disease. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Oral antibiotic therapy","Systemic antifungal therapy","Topical antifungal therapy alone","A more potent topical corticosteroid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — extensive, follicular or refractory infection warrants systemic therapy. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A patient has hyperkeratotic tinea pedis with markedly thickened soles. What may help in addition to an antifungal?","choices":["A systemic corticosteroid","An oral antihistamine","A topical antibiotic","A keratolytic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — added to the antifungal for hyperkeratotic disease. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 19-year-old has an annular scaly plaque on the forearm, and you decide to confirm it with potassium hydroxide microscopy. From where should the sample be taken?","choices":["From the active border of the lesion","From the cleared centre of the lesion","From normal skin two centimetres beyond the lesion","From a blood sample taken during the acute phase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the advancing edge carries the organism. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl has molluscum lesions in the genital area. Which is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Screening for sexually transmitted infections in every case as the sole action","Context-sensitive assessment, because location alone does not prove abuse","Immediate mandatory reporting on the basis of the lesion location alone","No assessment at all, because genital molluscum is expected in young children"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — location alone does not settle the question. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"A 68-year-old has a zoster eruption over the forehead and upper eyelid, with lesions on the tip of the nose. He reports eye pain and photophobia. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start systemic antiviral therapy immediately and arrange same-day ophthalmology evaluation","Arrange ophthalmology evaluation within two weeks and withhold antivirals until then","Start a topical antiviral eye ointment and review the patient in one week","Start a systemic corticosteroid alone and arrange same-day ophthalmology evaluation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — do not delay the antiviral while awaiting the eye review. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 64-year-old has thickened discoloured toenails and you decide to confirm the diagnosis before prescribing. Which tests apply, and how should the sample be taken?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide microscopy, periodic acid–Schiff stain of clippings, culture, or amplification testing; sample the most proximal accessible diseased nail bed","Potassium hydroxide microscopy or fungal culture only; sample the most distal free edge of the nail plate before any trimming of the onycholytic nail is performed","Serology for dermatophyte antibodies or a blood culture; no local sampling of the nail is required at any stage","A Wood lamp examination followed by biopsy of the nail matrix; sample only after six weeks of topical therapy has failed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — sample subungual debris after trimming the onycholytic nail. Sampling proximally is the part most often missed. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"You are covering a clinic and several patients with varicella are booked in. Which of these situations warrants prompt consultation?","choices":["A preadolescent child with a typical eruption and no fever","Any patient whose lesions have already crusted over completely","Any patient who has previously received one dose of vaccine","Pregnancy, neonatal exposure, immunocompromise, or severe complications"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — four named triggers for prompt consultation. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A colleague suggests incising and draining the swollen fingertip of a patient with herpetic whitlow. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Incise and drain it, because decompression shortens the course of the infection","Incise and drain it, but only after 72 hours of oral antiviral therapy have failed","Do not incise and drain it, because the lesion is sterile and will resolve within 24 hours","Do not incise and drain it, because that does not treat the virus and can delay healing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — explicitly contraindicated. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"A patient with dystrophic toenails asks for oral terbinafine. Which is the most appropriate next step before prescribing?","choices":["Confirm fungus first, because many dystrophic nails are not fungal","Prescribe the terbinafine, because the clinical appearance alone is diagnostic","Prescribe a topical agent instead, because oral therapy is never used for nails","Obtain a Wood lamp examination, because fluorescence confirms nail infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — many dystrophic nails are not fungal at all. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"An immunocompromised patient has had the first dose of recombinant zoster vaccine and needs faster completion. What interval is permitted for the second dose?","choices":["6 to 12 months after the first dose","At least 12 months after the first dose","1 to 2 months after the first dose","2 to 6 months after the first dose, with no shortening permitted"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a permitted shortening from the standard 2 to 6 months. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A patient with postherpetic neuralgia needs pain control. Which is the most appropriate first-line option?","choices":["Gabapentin or pregabalin, an appropriate tricyclic antidepressant, or topical lidocaine","A long-acting opioid titrated to effect over several months, with a capsaicin patch","A systemic corticosteroid tapered over three months","Continued antiviral therapy at a suppressive dose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a capsaicin patch may also help. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A patient is diagnosed with Ramsay Hunt syndrome and cannot fully close the right eye. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Start a systemic corticosteroid alone and arrange urgent ear, nose and throat or neurology evaluation, with no antiviral therapy and no corneal protection","Start antiviral therapy plus a systemic corticosteroid and arrange same-day ophthalmology evaluation for Hutchinson sign","Start antiviral therapy plus a systemic corticosteroid early when not contraindicated, arrange urgent ear, nose and throat or neurology evaluation, and protect the cornea","Start antiviral therapy alone and arrange routine ear, nose and throat review in six weeks, with no specific eye care"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — corneal protection matters when eyelid closure is impaired. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with candidal intertrigo is prescribed a topical azole. Beyond the antifungal, which environmental measures are needed?","choices":["Apply an occlusive dressing to the folds, avoid all washing and drying of the area, use a non-absorbent fabric, and treat incontinence only once the rash has settled","Soak the folds in warm water twice daily, apply talcum powder, and cover the area with a waterproof film","Expose the folds to direct sunlight daily, avoid moisture-wicking fabric, and treat incontinence only if it is severe","Gently dry the folds, reduce friction and occlusion, use moisture-wicking fabric or absorbent material, and address incontinence or hyperhidrosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — correcting the environment is listed first. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 62-year-old has a wart-like lesion on the dorsal hand that has been growing, bleeding, and has not responded to salicylic acid. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure the patient, since bleeding is expected in a resolving wart","Biopsy or refer, since bleeding, growing and refractory lesions warrant it","Repeat the same salicylic acid course for a further twelve weeks","Add a topical corticosteroid to reduce the bleeding and inflammation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — squamous cell carcinoma is in the differential. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"The parents of a 3-year-old with molluscum want a treatment they can apply at home. Which approved option fits?","choices":["Salicylic acid 40%, once daily, approved from age 2 years","Topical retinoid, once daily, approved from age 3 years","Berdazimer 10.3% gel, once daily, approved from age 1 year","Cantharidin 0.7%, once daily, approved from age 1 year"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the at-home option. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 136"},{"q":"A patient with zoster has severe disseminated disease with visceral involvement. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Intravenous acyclovir with specialist or hospital management","Oral valacyclovir at standard dose as an outpatient","A systemic corticosteroid alone with close outpatient review","Topical antiviral therapy with cool compresses and calamine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — also for central nervous system or sight-threatening disease. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 5-year-old has a scaly patch of alopecia. Before starting a prolonged systemic antifungal course, which investigations are appropriate?","choices":["A punch biopsy of the affected scalp in every patient before treatment is started","Potassium hydroxide microscopy and fungal culture when feasible","A Wood lamp examination alone, since a negative result excludes the diagnosis","Bacterial culture of the scalp in every patient before treatment is started"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — confirm before committing to prolonged oral therapy. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A patient has an atypical, widespread vesicular eruption and is immunocompromised. Which test is preferred, and from what sample?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide microscopy, from scale at the advancing border","Polymerase chain reaction, from vesicle fluid, a scab, or cells from the lesion base","Serology for varicella-zoster immunoglobulin M, from a venous blood sample","Viral culture, from a swab of intact skin adjacent to the eruption"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — preferred for atypical or immunocompromised presentations. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A Wood lamp examination is performed on a 20-year-old with suspected pityriasis versicolor and shows no fluorescence. Which is the most appropriate interpretation?","choices":["It excludes the diagnosis, because a coral-red fluorescence is expected instead","It confirms the diagnosis, because absence of fluorescence is the characteristic finding","It does not exclude the diagnosis, because the lamp's sensitivity is limited","It excludes the diagnosis, because all versicolor lesions fluoresce yellow-gold"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a yellow-gold fluorescence may be seen but is not reliable. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A man has a localized tinea cruris plaque. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["An oral azole such as ketoconazole, given for a minimum of twelve weeks","Topical nystatin applied twice daily until the plaque has fully resolved","A topical corticosteroid to relieve the itch, with an antifungal added only if it fails","A topical allylamine such as terbinafine, or a topical azole such as ketoconazole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — oral therapy is reserved for extensive or refractory disease. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
